  nonce: f2f6  # f2f6
  data:  # f2f6
    kind: invariant  # f2f6
    title: "method_starved_modules is module-granular but tests a per-kind property, so a module carrying BOTH call kinds can never be satisfied by a function-construct call — correct cataloguing causes withholding"  # f2f6
    status: violated  # f2f6
    priority: 1  # f2f6
    parent:  # f2f6
    tags:  # f2f6
    - io_boundary  # f2f6
    - verify_claims  # f2f6
    - false_inconclusive  # f2f6
    isbefore: []  # f2f6
    duplicate_of: []  # f2f6
    not_duplicate_of: []  # f2f6
    pr_ref:  # f2f6
    description: "FOUND 2026-08-26 while auditing std::fs for WI-bupor, and it BLOCKS that audit.\n\nTHE BLOCKER, stated first because it is the practical consequence. std::fs::File\nis catalogued today with three ASSOCIATED FUNCTIONS (open, create, create_new)\nand NOT ONE of its ~12 stable I/O METHODS (metadata, set_len, set_permissions,\nset_times, sync_all, sync_data, lock, lock_shared, try_lock, try_lock_shared,\nunlock). Adding those methods -- which is simply the correct catalogue content\nand is exactly what WI-bupor exists to do -- REGRESSES every Rust repo that\nopens a file without calling a File method. Measured live:\n\n    std::fs::File kinds TODAY: {'function'}          starved: []\n    after adding File::metadata as a method:          starved: ['std::fs::File']\n\nONE CORRECT ROW FLIPS A CLEAN VERDICT TO A WITHHELD ONE. So the predicate\nPENALISES CORRECT CATALOGUING, and the incentive it creates is to\nUNDER-catalogue -- to leave real I/O out so the module stays function-only.\nThat is the opposite of what the stdlib audit campaign is for.\n\nTHE MECHANISM. method_starved_modules (verify_claims.py:2953) computes\nmethod_modules as every module carrying ANY method-kind primitive, then for each\ncall edge marks the module `called`, and marks it `satisfied` ONLY when that\nedge's meta.call_construct == \"method\". It returns called - satisfied. The\ngranularity is THE MODULE, but the property it tests is PER-KIND. A module\ncarrying both kinds can never be satisfied by a function-construct call, even\nwhen the catalogue matched that call perfectly.\n\nThe docstring states the intent exactly, and the code does not implement it:\n\"when a repo calls into a method-keyed module and the analyzer produced no\nmethod-construct edge for it, the catalogue was never given anything it could\nmatch -- the analysis did not look.\" For a MIXED module the catalogue WAS given\nsomething it could match. The analysis did look.\n\nIT IS ALREADY FIRING, AND TODAY'S OWN FIX CREATED THREE OF THE CASES. PR #552\n(INV-nular) split rust's miskinded associated functions out of `methods:` into\n`functions:`. That fixed the MATCH but not the STARVATION, because the modules\nnow carry both kinds. Measured on the shipped catalogue with a\nfunction-construct call edge:\n\n    std::net::TcpStream    kinds=['function','method']  starved=YES  (connect)\n    std::net::UdpSocket    kinds=['function','method']  starved=YES  (bind)\n    std::process::Command  kinds=['function','method']  starved=YES  (new)\n    std::fs::File          kinds=['function']           starved=no   (open)\n\nstd::fs::File is clean ONLY because it is under-catalogued. The three that were\ncorrected today are the three that now starve. PR #552's closure note should be\nread with this correction: it fixed classification, not coverage.\n\nEIGHT MIXED-KIND MODULES SHIP TODAY across all catalogues -- rust 4 (above),\njavascript 2 (Deno, fs), python 1 (pathlib.Path), scala 1 (scala.io.Source).\npathlib.Path is the split made in the same PR #552, so python has the same\nexposure.\n\nPROPOSED FIX, not decided here. Evaluate starvation PER KIND rather than per\nmodule: a call edge should mark its module satisfied when the edge's construct\nmatches ANY kind the module declares, so a function-construct call into a module\nwith function-kind entries counts as \"the catalogue was given something it could\nmatch\". A module is then starved only when every call into it was of a construct\nthe catalogue cannot match -- which is the docstring's own stated intent.\n\nDIRECTION OF RISK, flagged because it decides the evidence bar: this is a\nGATE-LOOSENING change. Fewer starved modules means MORE clean verdicts, which is\nthe false-all-clear direction. The standing owner rule is that loosening needs\nits own cross-language measurement and never rides along with a fix pointing the\nother way. So this must NOT be bundled into WI-bupor's catalogue PR even though\nit is what unblocks it."  # f2f6
    fields:  # f2f6
      statement: "A module is method-starved only when every call into it used a construct the catalogue cannot match. A module declaring both function-kind and method-kind primitives must be satisfied by a function-construct call that matches its function-kind entries, and is not starved merely because no method-construct edge landed."  # f2f6
      root_cause: "method_starved_modules builds method_modules as every module carrying ANY method-kind primitive, then marks a module satisfied only when an edge's meta.call_construct == 'method'. The granularity is the module; the property is per-kind. Its own docstring states the intent the code does not implement."  # f2f6
- op: discuss  # 9e53
  at: '2026-08-26T23:38:24Z'  # 9e53
  by: agent  # 9e53
  actor: jgstern_agent  # 9e53
  clock: 2  # 9e53
  nonce: '9e53'  # 9e53
  message: "THIS ITEM NOW LEADS THE OWNER'S ARC. Ratified 2026-08-26, verbatim:\n\n  INV-soval -> Rust rows - WI-bupor -> WI-gihos -> WI-dadog, then WI-dukom\n  -> module_completeness grants -> cpp begins -> Stage 3, INV-vivok\n  -> then baseline measurements start, closing WI-tubij's window\n  -> Stage 4: INV-hosul -> WI-fosir only after WI-tubij -> P7\n\nIt supersedes the previous spelling (INV-pusin -> WI-lutuh + INV-zimud -> ...),\nwhose first two stages are now satisfied. This item was promoted to the head\nbecause it BLOCKS every catalogue-enrichment stage behind it: adding a correct\nmethod row to a function-only module flips a clean verdict to a withheld one,\nso no row can be written until it is fixed.\n\nTHE EVIDENCE BAR THIS ITEM CARRIES, and it is stricter than a normal fix.\nThis is a GATE-LOOSENING change: fewer starved modules means MORE clean\nverdicts, which is the false-all-clear direction. The standing owner rule is\nthat loosening needs its OWN cross-language measurement and never rides along\nwith a fix pointing the other way. So:\n  * it ships in its own PR, NOT bundled with WI-bupor's catalogue rows;\n  * it needs a measurement across languages on real repos, not a fixture-only\n    argument;\n  * the INV-nular signal must be PRESERVED -- a module declaring ONLY methods,\n    called with a function construct, must STILL starve. That is the defect\n    class this predicate was built to catch, and the fix must not blunt it.\n\nORDERING CONSTRAINTS RECORDED ELSEWHERE AND RESTATED HERE so the arc is legible\nfrom one place:\n  * WI-tubij (cross-language clock rows) must land BEFORE stage-3 baselines are\n    taken or AFTER they complete, never between -- it is recall-increasing and\n    would confound a precision measurement. No baselines are taken yet, so the\n    \"before\" window is OPEN and closes when stage 3 starts.\n  * WI-fosir must be measured AFTER WI-tubij: it is the 5-name cap on the\n    uncatalogued-module list, and clock rows shorten that list.\n  * INV-hosul and INV-vivok have NO upstream dependency.\n\nSCOPE HONESTY, recorded so nobody later reads the arc as claiming more than it\ndid: WI-lutuh cannot COMPLETE inside this arc. Fourteen languages beyond rust at\n30-50 modules each is weeks of audit. What this arc delivers is rust and cpp\ndone to the full evidence bar with the instruments proven, and the remaining\nlanguages left as decomposed items."  # 9e53
- op: discuss  # 3c98
  at: '2026-08-26T23:47:58Z'  # 3c98
  by: agent  # 3c98
  actor: jgstern_agent  # 3c98
  clock: 3  # 3c98
  nonce: 3c98  # 3c98
  message: "LOOSENING MEASUREMENT, as the standing rule requires for a gate that produces\nMORE clean verdicts. 42 real repos with surveys, language-stratified, from the\nWI-lutuh sweep. Artifacts: ~/hypergumbo_lab_notebook/soval_measure_08262026/\n\n  total starved modules summed over 42 repos\n    today            OLD=22   NEW=16   freed=6\n    post-WI-bupor    OLD=23   NEW=16   freed=7\n\n  NOTHING BECAME NEWLY STARVED. `new=[]` on every differing row, both arms.\n\nFREED, and every one is a MIXED-KIND module -- the population this item is\nabout. NOT ONE method-only module was freed, which is the safety property that\nmatters, because method-only is where the blind-language signal lives:\n    javascript  fs                      4 repos   kinds=[function, method]\n    scala       fs                      1 repo    (js sources inside a scala repo)\n    rust        std::process::Command   1 repo    kinds=[function, method]\n    rust        std::fs::File           post-WI-bupor only\n\nTHE DECISIVE ROW. Post-WI-bupor, std::fs::File starves under OLD (22 -> 23) and\ndoes NOT under NEW (16, unchanged). That is precisely what this item exists to\nbuy: WI-bupor's correct method rows can land without regressing a verdict.\n\nTWO CORRECTIONS TO MY OWN WORK ON THIS ITEM, recorded because each was a false\nsignal that looked like a result.\n\n1. THE FIRST FIX WAS CORRECT AND INSUFFICIENT. Satisfying on \"construct matches\n   any declared kind\" left std::fs::File starving, because CALL_CONSTRUCT IS NOT\n   STAMPED ON AN ASSOCIATED-FUNCTION CALL TO AN EXTERNAL TYPE. Measured: every\n   edge into std::fs::File / std::process::Command / std::path::Path across\n   three rust repos carries call_construct: None, while the same repos stamp it\n   on thousands of others (ripgrep: 917 method + 12 function of 1,156 external\n   call edges). A construct-only test cannot see that File::open was called.\n   So a SECOND satisfaction route was added: the called NAME matching a\n   function-kind primitive. The name is the evidence that survives an unstamped\n   edge.\n\n2. THE FIRST MEASUREMENT REPORTED A NULL, AND THE NULL WAS THE INSTRUMENT.\n   measure.py REIMPLEMENTED the predicate for both arms, so the NEW arm never\n   contained the fix and every repo read \"0 freed\". Rewritten to call the\n   PRODUCTION method_starved_modules for the NEW arm and keep a verbatim copy of\n   the shipped predicate for OLD -- A/B the tool, not a reimplementation. The\n   difference between the two runs is 0 freed versus 6.\n\nWHY THE NAME ROUTE DISCRIMINATES CORRECTLY, which is the part that makes this\nsafe rather than merely permissive:\n    Command::new   name matches a function-kind row  -> satisfied  (correct: the\n                   catalogue can match it; PR #552 declared it a function)\n    Path::new      no function-kind row              -> STARVES    (correct:\n                   Path::new performs no I/O and is deliberately absent)\n    java.io.File   constructor, no function-kind row -> STARVES    (correct:\n                   this IS the blind-language signal, kotlin and scala)\n\nWHAT THIS DOES NOT FIX, and it is the larger finding underneath. std::path::Path\nstarves in 3 of 3 rust repos and that is NOT addressed here. The rust analyzer\nemits its stdlib method calls with the module slot `external` rather than the\nstdlib module (INV-linub's L3 finding: 92.7% of 219 method edges carry slot\n`external`), so a `path.exists()` call never lands on std::path::Path and can\nnever satisfy it. That is INV-linub's territory, not this item's, and closing\nthis item must not be read as clearing rust starvation generally."  # 3c98
